          "final_text_tail": "k is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented deterministic parse fingerprinting for `ToolCalls` in `dspy/adapters/types/tool.py`:\n\n**Changes made:**\n1. **Import** `hashlib` (line 2).\n\n2. **`_canonicalize(value)`** helper (line 260) \u2014 recursively:\n   - sorts dict keys,\n   - converts tuples \u2192 lists,\n   - trims leading/trailing whitespace from strings,\n   - leaves numeric/boolean types unchanged (no coercion).\n\n3. **`_compute_fingerprint(name, args)`** helper (line 279) \u2014 builds `{\"name\": name, \"args\": _canonicalize(args)}`, serializes with `json.dumps(..., sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"))`, and returns the `sha256` hexdigest. The name is included in the representation but only `args` is canonicalized, per the spec.\n\n4. **`ToolCall`** class now has `fingerprint: str` and `id: str` fields (defaulting to `\"\"` so existing `ToolCall(**{\"name\":..., \"args\":...})` construction keeps working), computed once at creation in `model_post_init` (the Pydantic-idiomatic `__init__` hook):\n   - `.fingerprint` = full 64-char SHA-256 hex string\n   - `.id` = first 8 chars of the fingerprint\n\n**Verified:**\n- Determinism across arg key order, string whitespace, tuples\u2192lists, and nested dict ordering.\n- Fingerprints change when name or args values differ.\n- Exact match against a manual `json.dumps`/`sha256` computation.\n- All 26 existing `test_tool.py` tests and the broader adapter/reliability test suites (88 passed, 84 skipped) still pass \u2014 no behavior change to `format`, validation, or execution.",
